# x100-bridge settings. Both the bridge and x100ctl read this file.
# deploy/install.sh fills in the projector link and the API token for you.

# --- the projector: RS-232 or LAN, one of the two --------------------------------
# RS-232 through a USB adapter (projector: Setting > Advanced Settings > RS-232 Control > On).
# The /dev/serial/by-id/... name stays put across reboots; /dev/ttyUSB0 usually works too.
# X100_SERIAL=/dev/ttyUSB0
# X100_BAUD=115200
# ...or the projector's CONTROL port on your network (Setting > Network Settings >
# Wired LAN Control, and Standby LAN Control > On; give it a DHCP reservation):
# X100_HOST=
# X100_PORT=4661
X100_INPUTS_FILE=config/inputs.json

# The X100-4K says "on" when asked, even in standby, so the bridge works out standby, start-up
# and on from its other answers. These timings came from traces on a real one:
# X100_COOLDOWN_SECONDS=5     # after "off", hold back an "on" this long (5 s worked)
# X100_WARMUP_SECONDS=90      # allow this long for start-up (it took about a minute)
# X100_SETTLE_SECONDS=15      # once on, wait this long before commands that were waiting for it
# Asked things while it boots after a power cut, it hangs until it's unplugged (see the README):
# X100_POWER_UP_QUIET_SECONDS=300  # once it answers again (or this computer starts), say nothing this long
# X100_OUTAGE_PROBE_SECONDS=30     # while it isn't answering, ask only about power this often
# X100_START_HOLD_SECONDS=120      # after it starts, no power command this long ("off" waits)

# Pause between commands to the projector, in seconds. 0.1 is cautious and costs about
# 0.1 s per voice command (the bridge reads the power state before switching it). Once
# everything works, try 0.03; go back if the projector ever ignores a command.
# X100_MIN_GAP=0.1

# LAN only: if `x100ctl probe` says port 4661 never answers but `x100ctl on` works:
# X100_ONE_WAY=true
# X100_PJLINK_HOST=192.168.1.60        # gives one-way mode real power status
# X100_PJLINK_PASSWORD=

# --- local API: the voice listener sends its commands here -----------------------
# 127.0.0.1 when the listener runs on this machine (the Pi doing everything).
# 0.0.0.0 to accept the listener, or a phone shortcut, from your network.
LOCAL_API_BIND=127.0.0.1:8661
# Make one with `openssl rand -hex 16`; the same value goes in listener.env as BRIDGE_TOKEN.
# Required for 0.0.0.0, and install.sh sets one either way.
LOCAL_API_TOKEN=
# Seconds a command waits for the projector before the listener gets an error:
# LOCAL_API_TIMEOUT=10

# --- behaviour ---------------------------------------------------------------------
POLL_INTERVAL=30
# "switch to HDMI 2" turns the projector on first if it's off
AUTO_POWER_ON=true
LOG_LEVEL=INFO

# --- optional: Alexa through your own AWS account (see alexa/README.md) -----------
# alexa/infra/deploy.sh fills these in, and `install.sh --alexa` lets the bridge reach
# AWS. Leave AWS_IOT_ENDPOINT unset for local-only.
# THING_NAME=x100-projector
# AWS_IOT_ENDPOINT=
# AWS_IOT_PORT=8883
# CERT_FILE=certs/bridge.crt
# KEY_FILE=certs/bridge.key
# CA_FILE=certs/AmazonRootCA1.pem
# Remote keys as Alexa scenes, once `x100ctl key 0x92` proves they work:
# X100_SCENES={"Projector Focus": "0x92"}
